# [[ project_name ]] - Rill Agent Entry

This product is enrolled in Rill's native Codex factory, not the Rill source
repository. Use its own Git issues, Pull Requests, current revision and
`.rill/state.json` as the handoff record. Never invent an approval or green CI.

1. At session start inspect `rill status`, `rill doctor` and the active issue.
   Read `.rill/symbols.txt`, then targeted `rg -n` ranges before large files.
2. For independent module discovery, coordinator may dispatch 2-3 read-only
   explorers concurrently. Their replies are bounded source locations.
3. Parallel workers require independent module contexts and disjoint file
   ownership. A worker implements code and corresponding tests together;
   never split coder and tester responsibilities across agents.
4. Native Codex agents allow at most 3 child threads and depth 1. Never spawn
   nested agents, auto-upgrade models or create a parallel task database.
5. Never delete, skip, weaken or fake assertions. Test existing and new
   behavior and retain real type, integration and security coverage.
6. Only after `just ci-full` exits 0 may the coordinator dispatch one read-only
   reviewer, when the Git diff exceeds 30 changed lines or touches core,
   configuration, security, permissions or workflows.
7. Reviewer reads `REVIEW.md` first and reviews only the candidate diff;
   findings require `path:line` evidence and false-positive checking.
   With no serious findings it replies exactly `LGTM`.
8. Stop after at most TWO reviewer/fix rounds. If the second still fails,
   halt and provide the owner with the exact blocker; do not burn more quota.
9. Existing product-owned config stays intact except by explicitly approved
   `rill upgrade`. Rill file presence does not prove native Hook execution.
10. Never bypass Codex approvals or publish, deploy, merge, spend or delete
    unrelated work without explicit owner authorization. Use the installed
    `rill-deliver`, `rill-review` and `rill-recover` Skills as appropriate.
